|> Exercise
    handle=_secant_cosecant_intersections_

    In the drawing below, at what coordinates,
    expressed as a function of $\theta$,
    does the tangent to the circle
    intersect the $x$ and $y$ axes?

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_theta_tangent.svg

    |> Solution

        For starters assume that $\theta$ lies in
        the first quadrant, in
        which case both coordinates are nonnegative, and, more specifically, 
        are equal to the two 
        purple lengths below:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_theta_tangent_Q1.svg

        Regarding this diagram, note that:

        |> List
            style=gap:1em

            we have already “used up” the fact that
            the radius of the circle is $1$ by
            making the dropped height (which used to
            be a radius) length $1$;

            we use a “the complement of my complement
            is myself”-type argument to deduce that the
            upper left angle is $\theta$, as well
            (cf. Exercise >>_complement_calculation_,
            Exercise >>_complement_of_complement_).

        Because $1 \cdot \sec(\theta) = \sec(\theta)$ the
        bottom length is $\sec(\theta)$ while the vertical
        length is $1\cdot \csc(\theta) = \csc(\theta)$, similarly:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_theta_tangent_Q1_first_solve.svg

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_theta_tangent_Q1_second_solve.svg

        ...i.e., in the first quadrant, at least, the
        coordinates are $\sec(\theta)$ and $\csc(\theta)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_theta_tangent_Q1_with_answer.svg

        For the other quadrants we can reason by
        symmetry with the first quadrant:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_theta_tangent_all_quadrants.svg

        In more detail, the product of the two coordinates
        marked by purple arrows is the same in all four
        quadrants, because “minus times minus is plus”; but
        in first quadrant, the product is $1$, because
        we know the product takes the form $\cos(\theta)\cdot\sec(\theta)$
        in that quadrant;
        therefore the product is $1$ in _all_ quadrants; but
        the coordinate marked by the inside purple arrow is
        $\cos(\theta)$ in all quadrants; therefore, lastly,
        the coordinate marked by the _outside_ purple arrow
        is $\sec(\theta)$ in all quadrants, since $\sec(\theta)$
        is the unique number whose product with $\cos(\theta)$
        is $1$; similarly the inside orange arrow marks coordinate
        $\sin(\theta)$ is all quadrants, so the outside orange
        arrow (by similar reasoning) has coordinate $\csc(\theta)$
        in all quadrants, QED. (I.e., the grand conclusion is that the
        two coordinates are

        $$
        \Huge \sec(\theta)
        $$

        for the $x$-axis intersection, and

        $$
        \Huge \csc(\theta)
        $$

        for the $y$-axis intersection, no matter the quadrant.)